PATCH 554: c33c's phase handler 0xc38b wrote its record through stack residue.

`FUN_0000_c33c` walks a table of ten phase handlers at DGROUP:0xe6f5 and dispatches each one as

    c36f: ff 97 f5 e6      call *0xe6f5(%bx)

-- a NEAR call with no arguments, ES:DI simply inherited from 0x378e, which sets it once:

    3796: 8c d8 / 8e c0    mov %ds,%ax ; mov %ax,%es      ; ES = DS = DGROUP
    379e: bf be 6b         mov $0x6bbe,%di                ; the record cursor

Four of the ten handlers write through that cursor (0x5fb0, 0x6016, 0xc9af, 0xc38b), each advancing DI
for the next.  Patches 310 and 526 established the port's model for it -- the shim global
`g_fist_render_di`, published by c33c and advanced by every writer -- and converted three of the four.
0xc38b was the one left, and it still deref'd Ghidra's invented `param_2` as a host pointer.  Since the
dispatch passes no arguments, `param_2` is stack residue.  Measured on AZER7:

    Program received signal SIGSEGV
    0x08086a48 in FUN_0000_c38b (param_1=3460, param_2=0x81d2ff4) at build/fist.c:33350
    33350       *param_2 = 0x1340;
    g_mem = 0x81d3260 ; param_2 - g_mem = -620

620 bytes BEFORE the address space.  Patch 526's own header had already named this function's problem
in as many words ("c33c dispatches its handlers with NO arguments, so param_1 ... is garbage").

## Why it only surfaced now

The write was always wrong; it was unreachable.  The guard is

    c392: 80 3e 49 15 1e   cmpb $0x1e,0x1549

and DGROUP:0x1549 was declared `undefined2`, so the port's `DAT_1000_d549 == '\x1e'` also required byte
0x154a to be zero -- and 0x154a is a live counter with twelve of its own references.  Patch 553
narrowed 0x1549 to the byte all fourteen of its references use, which made the branch reachable for the
first time and turned a latent out-of-bounds write into a visible SEGV.  Verified by bisect: with the
553 tree the crash reproduces, and it does not on the pre-547 tree.

That is the patch working as intended.  The fix is this function, not un-narrowing 0x1549.

## The three defects

(a) the record write and the `stos` cursor -- described above;
(b) BASE LOSS: `*(byte *)(DAT_2000_3ae0 + 0x1b)` deref'd a DGROUP near offset as a host pointer, where
    the asm reads `movzbw 0x1b(%si)` = byte[DGROUP:si+0x1b].  (`DAT_2000_3ae0` is declared `int **`,
    which is how it got there.)  On the [0x6d2c]&0x80 path only, so it had not fired yet;
(c) the RETURN CONTRACT: both exits do `mov $0xffff,%si`, and the carry is the phase result -- `clc` =
    found, so c33c stops; `stc` = empty, so c33c advances to the next phase.  Ghidra dropped both.
    c33c presets `g_fist_cf = 1` before the call and reads it after, so only the found path clears it.

The rewritten body follows the asm's control flow exactly, including its single fall-through at 0xc3bc,
rather than duplicating the emit block down two arms.

## NOT fixed here

`caab`'s second argument is BP -- `cad5: mov %bp,%ax ; ab stos %ax,%es:(%di)`, landing in record field
+0x1a.  c38b does not set BP, and caab has exactly one caller in the whole image (this one), so BP is
inherited from above 0x378e and is stack residue in the port as well.  It is passed through unchanged
rather than replaced with an invented value, so this patch changes nothing about that field.
board:0014 carries it.

board:0014

